This file lists all bug fixes, changes, etc., made since the 
second edition of the AWK book was published in September 2023.

Sep 29, 2026
	More bracket expression fixes in b.c.  Collating elements and
	equivalence classes work anywhere in a class rather than only
	when they make up the whole of one, and both spellings failed
	the same way: the character was dropped and the rest of the
	class kept, so [a[.b.]c] did not match the b, and where the
	form came last the whole class collapsed to that one character,
	so [ab[=c=]] matched only the c.  [^[.x.]] lost its negation,
	and neither could end a range.  An invalid class name is a
	syntax error now, as in gawk, rather than quietly accepted.

	Three out of bounds accesses while lexing a class are fixed as
	well, silent in the same way as the two above: writes that a
	[:class:] expansion could carry past the end of the buffer, an
	over-read where a truncated [. or [= had no terminator to find,
	and another where a backslash was the last byte of the class.

	HAT, the marker for ^ in a class, was NCHARS+2 == U+04ED, an
	ordinary Cyrillic letter, so that one character matched neither
	/./ nor any negated class while a class holding it matched
	every line.  The values b.c reserves for itself now sit above
	the last code point.

	More tests in testdir/T.ccl and testdir/T.utfre.

Sep 29, 2026
	Bracket expression fixes in b.c.  A class left with no members
	matches nothing instead of everything, an empty one being how
	the parser represents the empty regular expression: /[\0]/ and
	/[\x]/ matched every line.  A NUL is allowed as the left end of
	a range.  An endpoint past the last code point is clamped
	rather than expanded, which used to ask for millions of members
	and overflow bufsz on the way.

	Two defects that only a checking tool shows: the terminator of
	the member array was written one element past the allocation
	whenever a class held exactly bufsz members, and the
	accumulator reading \u overflowed a signed int.  An ordinary
	malloc hides the first, since the element is read straight back.

	New tests in testdir/T.ccl and testdir/T.utfre.

Sep 29, 2026
	u8_rune() no longer attempts to decode bytes in a multibyte
	locale that is not utf-8.  Any pair of bytes in such an encoding
	that was accidentally well formed utf-8 was decoded into an
	unrelated character.  For example, 1920 of GB18030's two byte
	characters were read as one character rather than two bytes, and
	incorrectly: d2 bb is U+4E00, which awk decoded as U+04BB.

	New test in testdir/T.nonutf8.

Sep 29, 2026
	fnematch() kept awk_mb_cur_max bytes of lookahead while
	u8_rune() reads up to four, so if awk_mb_cur_max was less than
	four then a four byte utf-8 sequence in the input was decoded
	partly from bytes past the logical end of the buffer.  For
	example, with U+10000 as RS, ja_JP.SJIS and ja_JP.eucJP read a
	two record file as one.

	New test in testdir/T.rslookahead.

Sep 24, 2026
	u8_rune() no longer accepts a sequence that encodes no
	character: one above the last code point, a utf-16 surrogate, or
	an overlong form.  Each is returned a byte at a time, as any
	other byte that is not utf-8 always has been.  An overlong NUL
	decoded as a rune 0, which the regular expression code reads as
	the end of the string, so no pattern could match past one while
	index() and substr() read through it.  length() and a match also
	disagreed about such a record: on a, C0 80, b, length() counted
	three characters where /./ matched two.

	u8_isutf() and u8_rune() no longer call strlen() to learn
	whether two, three or four bytes are available.  That cost a
	pass over the rest of the record for every character decoded,
	which made length(), split(), substr(), a match and gsub() all
	quadratic in the length of a multibyte record.

	New tests in testdir/T.utf8bad.

